Poll for interrupts from deparse's output buffer.

deparse never checks for interrupts, and its output for a nested value
grows quadratically with the depth: every line is indented two spaces
per level, and print2buff measures the whole line again for each token
it appends.  A list a hundred thousand levels deep, which an input can
build in a loop within the eval target's budget, deparses for minutes;
as.character() of a list or of a closure reaches it.  On the main
thread's 8 MiB stack the recursion overflowed first, so this was hidden
behind a stack-overflow crash until the harnesses moved R to a thread
with a 1 GiB stack (harnesses/common.h).

Poll every 1024 appends, through R_UnwindProtect so the line buffer,
which deparse mallocs, is freed when the poll unwinds; a leak there
would be reported as a crash.  R_CheckUserInterrupt also checks the C
stack, so deparse recursion that does reach the thread's limit ends in
R's "C stack usage" error rather than a sanitizer fault.

Instrumentation, not a fix: R's deparse is only this slow on values no
program builds, and this changes no result.  Without it the harness
reports a timeout for such an input rather than a wrong answer.

diff --git a/src/main/deparse.c b/src/main/deparse.c
index bcc3641..0eb0ca5 100644
--- a/src/main/deparse.c
+++ b/src/main/deparse.c
@@ -1572,9 +1572,33 @@ static void writeline(LocalParseData *d)
     d->startline = true;
 }
 
+/* Fuzzing instrumentation: deparse never polls for interrupts, and its
+   output for a nested value is quadratic in the depth (two spaces of
+   indentation per level on every line, and the line measured again for
+   every token appended), so the fuzz harnesses' work budget cannot stop
+   it.  Poll every 1024 appends.  The poll may unwind, so the line buffer
+   is freed on the way out rather than leaked. */
+static SEXP deparse_poll(void *data)
+{
+    (void) data;
+    R_CheckUserInterrupt();
+    return R_NilValue;
+}
+
+static void deparse_poll_cleanup(void *data, Rboolean jump)
+{
+    if (jump)
+	R_FreeStringBuffer((R_StringBuffer *) data);
+}
+
 static void print2buff(const char *strng, LocalParseData *d)
 {
     size_t tlen, bufflen;
+    static unsigned int appends = 0;
+
+    if ((++appends & 1023) == 0)
+	R_UnwindProtect(deparse_poll, NULL,
+			deparse_poll_cleanup, &(d->buffer), NULL);
 
     if (d->startline) {
 	d->startline = false;
